Make the workbench layout viewport-aware

The workbench is unconditionally desktop-first: `layout.ts` has no viewport,
touch, or breakpoint awareness, and `browser/media/style.css` has no width
media query. On a phone the activity bar, sidebar, editor, panel, and status
bar all compete for one small viewport, which is the actual cause of the
workbench being unusable there.

This adds `WorkbenchViewportPolicy`, a dependency-free policy object that
classifies container geometry as `phone`, `tablet`, or `desktop` and exposes
that as observables. `Layout#layout()` feeds it the current main container
width and height on every pass and toggles a `phone-layout` class on the main
container, so classification tracks rotation, window resize, and a Samsung
DeX plug or unplug live — no reload and no user-agent sniffing.

Classification uses geometry on purpose. Code's own phone layout in
`vs/sessions` gates on the `isMobile` user-agent flag, which is wrong in both
directions for code-server: it denies a constrained desktop browser a usable
layout, and Samsung DeX keeps the Android `Mobi` user agent even on an
external display. A short landscape viewport remains compact while a wide
external display becomes desktop. `setOverride` exists so a user-facing
setting can pin the class later.

`phoneLayout.css` carries the compact rules, every one of them scoped to
`.monaco-workbench.phone-layout`, so desktop rendering is unchanged by
construction. It raises action items to a 44x44 px touch target, sets
`touch-action: manipulation`, and contains overscroll.

